description:
## Location
rust/lichen-core/src/loadng.rs lines 483-526

## Issue
Unlike RREP which has `receive_rrep` (line 338) enforcing signature presence at the API level, RERR parsing provides no equivalent validation helper. The doc comment at lines 490-494 states:

> Unsigned REERRs SHOULD be rejected by the receiving stack to prevent spoofed route deletions. `None` here means signature was absent in the wire format; callers MUST verify presence before acting on route invalidation.

However, callers using `Rerr::from_bytes()` must remember to manually check `signature.is_some()` before processing. This is an API design gap that invites security bugs.

## Attack scenario
An attacker sends an unsigned RERR claiming a valid route is unreachable. If the receiving stack forgets to check `signature.is_some()`, it deletes the route. This enables denial-of-service via route deletion without cryptographic authentication.

## Suggested fix
Add a `validate_signature` method or a `receive_rerr` helper analogous to `receive_rrep`:

```rust
impl Rerr {
    /// Validate RERR has required signature for route invalidation.
    /// Link layer performs cryptographic verification; this checks presence.
    pub fn require_signature(&self) -> Result<&Self, LoadngError> {
        if self.signature.is_some() {
            Ok(self)
        } else {
            Err(LoadngError::MissingSignature)
        }
    }
}
```

Or parse into two types: `UnsignedRerr` and `SignedRerr`, making the trust boundary visible in the type system.
